Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Infra Substrates", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell["\<\
The substrate is the graph the observer lives on. The named example \
substrates come at three sizes, with the style they are drawn in and the code \
that builds them. The tessellation graphs are the regular and uniform maps: a \
Platonic solid, a flat torus or a hyperbolic quotient, and the ball cut from \
an infinite tiling of the plane, the hyperbolic plane or the sphere. A \
tessellation carries its combinatorial curvature, Euler characteristic and \
genus, which say what surface the graph is. A uniform-length graph is the \
contact graph of a hard-sphere packing of a region, a manifold discretised so \
that its edges have nearly equal length; fractals and trees complete the \
examples. A substrate is prepared by cutting it to its centre or removing its \
rim, and any substrate can be inflated by growing a fiber of new vertices \
over every vertex. Fibered substrates are the same catalogue for fibrations.\
\>", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

